  "rubric_items": [
    {"id": "DS-R1", "score": 5, "reason": "The design reuses AppointmentService.get, DoctorRepo.get/availability, withinAvailability and the configured zone. The rule stays in the service, as the layering requires. There is no new endpoint, table, migration or infrastructure, and SUMMARY_SELECT is left alone per A-06."},
    {"id": "DS-R2", "score": 5, "reason": "Every story AC maps to a behaviour. S-1 AC3/AC4/AC5 are covered by starts_at &gt;= now, no cap and the hospital zone. S-2 AC2 works because the Inactive notice does not depend on start time. S-4 works because the flag is false and notices render only in the Scheduled branch."},
    {"id": "DS-R3", "score": 5, "reason": "Both new fields on all six endpoints have a name, type, nullability, format and example. The outsideAvailability condition is stated exactly enough to test."},
    {"id": "DS-R4", "score": 4, "reason": "Choices are cited or decided by A-07/A-08, and the cited lines match the code. Gap: doctorStatus is declared not null, but DoctorRepo.get can return null, and the design never says why the doctor always exists or how null is handled."},
    {"id": "DS-R5", "score": 5, "reason": "The trade-offs are explained: extra queries vs. changing SUMMARY_SELECT, the action endpoints gaining fields additively, the outsideAvailability name clashing with the doctor detail's id list, and service-vs-route placement."},
    {"id": "DS-R6", "score": 4, "reason": "Clear and consistent overall. One slip: CO-8 bundles a web page under the api module."}
  ],
